speechtext:
Mr. LaVERGNE:
]. Is station CHRC at Quebec state-owned or privately owned?
2.	If the station is privately owned, who are the owners?

If there is any cause for alarm, it has existed for all the years the Canadian Pacific railway has been in operation. I checked up this matter because I must confess that I was a little surprised to learn of it. The Canadian Pacific Railway has never had to apply to the railway commission in connection with the abandonment of branch lines. The section which my hon. friend just read refers to divisional points, and so far as employees can be protected in connection with divisional points, they are protected by the clause with which we are dealing. I think it was upon the suggestion of a labour representative in the Senate that this subsection (a) was included. It reads:
(a) that part o.f section one hundred and seventy-nine of the Railway Act which relates to compensation of employees for financial loss caused to them by removal, closing or abandonment of any railway station or divisional point;
The employees are still protected just as they have been in the past. Throughout the years the Canadian Pacific Railway have had the right to abandon lines if they so chose without submission to anyone, whereas the Canadian National has not.
